  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sqlf_131",
      "name": "nulls-three-valued-logic-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Predict what NULL does to a filter",
      "description": "Count the rows four NULL-sensitive filters keep on a small customers table, then pick the expressions that evaluate to UNKNOWN.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The customers table has 11 rows. Their referral_source values are:\n\n'ads', 'friend', NULL, 'search', 'ads', 'friend', NULL, 'friend', 'ads', NULL, 'search'\n\nHow many rows does SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers WHERE ... return for each of these filters, in this order?\n\n(a) referral_source <> 'ads'\n(b) NOT (referral_source = 'ads')\n(c) referral_source NOT IN ('ads', NULL)\n(d) referral_source IS NOT 'ads'",
          "explanation": "There are 3 'ads' rows, 5 rows with another source and 3 NULL rows. (a) For a NULL row, NULL <> 'ads' is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE, so only the 5 known non-ads rows remain. (b) NOT of UNKNOWN is still UNKNOWN, so rewriting the test with NOT changes nothing: 5. (c) x NOT IN ('ads', NULL) means x <> 'ads' AND x <> NULL. The second part is UNKNOWN for every row, so the whole condition is FALSE (for 'ads') or UNKNOWN (for everything else) and never TRUE: 0 rows. (d) IS NOT is SQLite's null-safe comparison (IS DISTINCT FROM in standard SQL and PostgreSQL): NULL IS NOT 'ads' is TRUE, so the 5 known rows and the 3 NULL rows are kept: 8.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "(a) 5, (b) 5, (c) 0, (d) 8",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "(a) 8, (b) 8, (c) 8, (d) 8",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "(a) 5, (b) 8, (c) 0, (d) 8",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "(a) 5, (b) 5, (c) 5, (d) 8",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Which of these expressions evaluate to UNKNOWN (SQLite returns NULL for UNKNOWN)? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "A comparison with =, <>, <, > is UNKNOWN as soon as one side is NULL, even NULL <> NULL: two unknown values are not known to be different. NOT UNKNOWN stays UNKNOWN, so NOT (NULL = 'ads') is UNKNOWN too. 'search' NOT IN ('ads', 'friend', NULL) expands to 'search' <> 'ads' AND 'search' <> 'friend' AND 'search' <> NULL, which is TRUE AND TRUE AND UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN. The others have a definite answer: IS NOT is SQLite's null-safe comparison, so NULL IS NOT 'friend' is TRUE; FALSE AND anything is FALSE; TRUE OR anything is TRUE (1 = 1 is TRUE); and 'friend' IN ('ads', 'friend', NULL) is TRUE because one comparison already matched, and TRUE OR UNKNOWN is TRUE.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "NULL <> NULL",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "NULL IS NOT 'friend'",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "FALSE AND NULL",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "1 = 1 OR NULL",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "NOT (NULL = 'ads')",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "'friend' IN ('ads', 'friend', NULL)",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "'search' NOT IN ('ads', 'friend', NULL)",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
